@{
    Layout = null;
}
@*
    ONE SENTENCE FOR FOUR CAUSES: expired, already used, belonging to a booking that can no longer
    be cancelled, or never issued at all.

    Telling them apart is exactly what an attacker wants — "already used" says a booking exists and
    somebody cancelled it; "expired" says a booking exists and roughly when it was; "never issued"
    says the guess was wrong. A page that distinguished them would rebuild, at the end of the flow,
    the enumeration oracle this design removed at the start by never accepting a reference.

    It costs a genuine booker some precision, and that is the trade: they are told to contact the
    site, which is the position they were in before the feature existed.
*@
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <title>This link can no longer be used</title>
</head>
<body>
    <main class="cancel">
        <h1>This link can no longer be used</h1>
        <p>
            Cancellation links stop working once they have been used, and once the booking they
            belong to has started.
        </p>
        <p>If you still need to change or cancel a booking, please contact us.</p>
    </main>
</body>
</html>
